=base:summary.cmark
The fifth iteration of the language is out. Numbers are an integer and a float, a template can compute, sort a list and format a number, and all six implementations run the whole conformance corpus. Some existing output changes.

=base:main-content.cmark

# tramaj v5: numbers that compute, in all six implementations

tramaj has gone through four iterations so far: v1 (the original
block/template split), v2 (expression-oriented documents, static analyses
and the `Node` JSON format), v3 (symbols and constraints) and v4 (types).
This is the fifth. It changes what a number is, and lets a template do
something with one:

- **Two number types.** An integer and a float, with no implicit conversion
  between them.
- **Arithmetic.** Ten named builtins, `round` among them, in an optional
  profile that a host turns on.
- **Sorting.** `sort-by` and `sort-by-descending` order a list by a key
  function.
- **Number formatting.** `format-number` writes a number with a fixed
  number of decimals and a group separator, rounded the same way everywhere.

And the part that makes it a version rather than a design: **all six
implementations have all of it, and run the same corpus.**

```
@rows=sort-by-descending($ctx.rows, (r) => $r.spend)
@total=sum(map($rows, (r) => $r.spend))
.table(
  map($rows, (r) => .tr(.td($r.name), .td(format-number($r.spend, 2, ","))))
  , .tr(.td("total"), .td(format-number($total, 2, ","))))
```

## What changed since v4

**Numbers.** A JSON number in the context is typed by its text: `3` is an
integer, `3.0` and `3e0` are floats. A template literal follows the same
rule. Integers are exact over a guaranteed range; an integer outside it is
refused instead of being rounded. Comparisons do not mix the two types, and
`real(…)`, `floor(…)` and `round(…)` convert when the template asks.

**Arithmetic.** Until v4 the reference said a template "compares and
selects, it does not compute". That is reversed, narrowly: the grammar has
no infix operators, and arithmetic is ten named builtins (`sum`, `product`,
`negate`, `round`, …) in a profile the host enables. A host that does not
enable it can refuse such a program up front, from its static analysis,
without evaluating it. The design and the alternatives that were rejected
are in [Two designs accepted](/design-arithmetic-and-shapes.html).

**Sorting and formatting.** `sort-by(list, fn)` and
`sort-by-descending(list, fn)` order a list by the key `fn` gives each
element, and are stable. `format-number(x, decimals, group)` writes
`1234567.891` as `"1,234,567.89"`. Both are part of the core language and
need no profile. The rounding rule of `format-number` is normative, because
the native formatters of the six host languages disagree on ties and on
negative zero: each implementation writes the rule by hand rather than
calling its own formatter.

All of this is specified in
[`reference.md`](/reference-language.html) §3, §11 and §13, and recorded,
with the alternatives, in
[`specs/decisions.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/specs/decisions.md)
sections 18 and 20.

## Breaking changes

The arithmetic profile is an addition: a template that does not use it is
unaffected. The number split is not optional, and it changes the output of
some existing templates and hosts:

<table>
<tr><th>what</th><th>v4</th><th>v5</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>str</code> of a whole-valued float</td><td><code>str(1.0)</code> is <code>1</code></td><td><code>str(1.0)</code> is <code>1.0</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>eq</code> across the two types</td><td><code>eq(1, 1.0)</code> is <code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lt</code>, <code>lte</code>, <code>gt</code>, <code>gte</code> across the two types</td><td><code>gt(1.5, 0)</code> is <code>true</code></td><td>a <code>TypeMismatch</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>a JSON number in the context</td><td>a double, whatever its text</td><td><code>3</code> is an integer, <code>3.0</code> and <code>3e0</code> are floats</td></tr>
<tr><td>an integer outside the range</td><td>rounded to the nearest double</td><td>refused: a parse error as a literal, a <code>TypeMismatch</code> in the context</td></tr>
<tr><td>the object key <code>"$term"</code></td><td>an ordinary key</td><td>reserved, like <code>"$sym"</code> and <code>"$type"</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the type primitive <code>number</code> (v4 types)</td><td>a primitive</td><td>gone: <code>int</code> and <code>float</code> replace it</td></tr>
</table>

[Migrating to integers and floats](/migrating-to-integers-and-floats.html)
takes each row in turn: who it affects, how to find the templates it
affects, and what to change.

## Every implementation runs the whole corpus

The [conformance corpus](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/tree/main/corpus)
holds 646 cases: a template, a context and the expected output, compared
byte for byte, in both `concrete` and `symbolic` mode. Until now an
implementation could skip a case that needed a profile it did not provide,
and while the ports caught up, some did. That is over: `"int-float"` and
`"arithmetic"` are now required of every runner, and a case that lists one
fails, rather than being skipped, in an implementation without it.

The only cases any implementation still skips are the ones written for the
other integer range. PureScript and TypeScript have the guaranteed 53-bit
range, because their host number is a double; the other four have the
64-bit range. Each skips the few cases that pin down the other range's
edges.

<table>
<tr><th>implementation</th><th>integer range</th><th>corpus</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj</code> (PureScript)</td><td>53-bit</td><td>passes; skips only the 64-bit range cases</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-hs</code> (Haskell)</td><td>64-bit</td><td>passes; skips only the 53-bit range cases</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-rs</code> (Rust)</td><td>64-bit</td><td>passes; skips only the 53-bit range cases</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-js</code> (TypeScript)</td><td>53-bit</td><td>passes; skips only the 64-bit range cases</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-go</code> (Go)</td><td>64-bit</td><td>passes; skips only the 53-bit range cases</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-py</code> (Python)</td><td>64-bit</td><td>passes; skips only the 53-bit range cases</td></tr>
</table>

## Packages

Where to get v5 today:

<table>
<tr><th>package</th><th>language</th><th>where</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-purs</code>, <code>tramaj-halogen</code></td><td>PureScript</td><td>PureScript registry, 0.4.1</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-cli</code></td><td>PureScript</td><td>from the repository</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-hs</code></td><td>Haskell</td><td>Hackage, 0.4.0.0</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-rs</code>, <code>tramaj-cli-rs</code></td><td>Rust</td><td>crates.io, 0.2.0</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj</code> (<code>tramaj-py</code>)</td><td>Python</td><td>PyPI, 0.2.0</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>@lucasdicioccio/tramaj-js</code>, <code>@lucasdicioccio/tramaj-react</code></td><td>TypeScript</td><td>from the repository; npm has only a placeholder so far</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-go</code></td><td>Go</td><td>from the repository, as <code>github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/tramaj-go</code>, at a commit on <code>main</code></td></tr>
</table>

Each package has its own [changelog](/topics/changelog.html) page and Atom
feed. [Integrating tramaj](/integrate-tramaj.html) says how to depend on
each one.

## What's next

v5 is a fifth iteration, not a stable release: the language can still
change. The [roadmap](/roadmap.html) lists what is wanted next: the npm
packages and folds to more rendering targets. Beyond v5,
[`?shape`](/design-arithmetic-and-shapes.html) is an accepted design for
allocating a structure of symbols in one form; no implementation has it
yet.

